  {
    "description": "Read the recipe that draws a target in this design (its look, or the default one) or a look by ID or name, with the size it is usually built at and its parameters (what params_set can change per object). Start from it to change what something looks like.",
    "examples": [],
    "input": {
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "look": {
          "description": "Look ID or name",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "target": {
          "description": "What a look draws: 'furniture:<type>' (a furniture type), 'opening:<door|sliding-door|gate|window|opening|glass-block>', 'element:<stair|pond|drain>' or 'wall:railing'",
          "pattern": "^(furniture|opening|element|wall):[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "type": "object",
    },
    "kind": "query",
    "name": "look_get",
    "permission": "read",
    "runtime": "any",
    "title": "Read a look",
  },
  {
    "description": "Add a look to the design: a recipe that draws furniture, doors and windows, or elements, like a font draws characters. Everyone who opens the design sees it. With use, every object of those targets switches to it; otherwise set it per object with look_set. Recipe format: { params?: {name: declaration}, seed?, script, materials? } where script is a JSONata expression (jsonata.org) returning a list of parts (nested lists are flattened), or { parts, plan } to draw its own plan symbol. Millimetres, angles in degrees. The script sees the object's size as $w, $d, $h and each parameter as $name (its default, or the value an object sets). Each parameter is declared with JSON Schema keywords: { type ("string" | "number" | "integer" | "boolean", or a list with "null" to let the script work it out), default (required; null only with "null" among the types), format? ("material" for a material id), enum? (the allowed values), enumTitles? ([{zh?, en?}] naming each enum value), minimum?, maximum?, title? ({zh?, en?}, shown to people; at least the project language), description? (English) }. Declare a parameter for whatever a person may want to change without a new look: materials of the main surfaces (format material with an enum of fitting materials), decoration on/off, a few shape choices (enum). Local frame: origin at the centre of the footprint on the floor (an opening: the centre of its sill; x along the wall, z across it), x across w, z across d with +z the front, y up. JSONata basics: ( $a := 1; $b := $a * 2; [ ... ] ) for local variables; [0..$n - 1].( $i := $; ... ) to repeat; cond ? a : b; = != < <= > >= and or $not(); + - * / %; $min([a, b]) $max([...]) $abs $floor $ceil $sqrt $power $round (half up) and our $sin $cos $tan $atan2 (degrees) $hypot $clamp(x, lo, hi) $lerp(a, b, t) $rnd(k) (pseudo-random 0..1, fixed per seed) $pi; $f := function($x) { ... } for helpers of your own. Always use $w, $d, $h and $variables: inside [list].( ... ) a bare name refers to the current item. Parts; mat is a material: one of the default library's (fabric cushion linen bedding canvas canvas2 wood woodDark birch white red cabinet countertop ceramic ceramicShade appliance plaster stone stoneDark terracotta soil iron dark glass book1 book2 book3 book4 flower tube bark leaf leaf2 carBody carGlass tyre metal headlight taillight frame glassBlock wall water grate oak teak tile antislip redtile paver terrazzo gravel cement marble slate grass paint greybrick redbrick stonewall woodcladding concrete) or one the recipe defines in materials: {id: glTF 2.0 material {name: {zh?, en?}, pbrMetallicRoughness: {baseColorFactor: [r, g, b, a] linear 0..1, metallicFactor, roughnessFactor}, alphaMode?: OPAQUE | BLEND}}: $box([w, h, d], [x, y, z] centre, mat, r? edge radius, [rx, ry, rz]?); $block([w, h, d], x, y0 bottom, z, mat, r?); $cylinder(r, h, [x, y, z] centre, mat, {r2? top radius, axis? x|y|z, seg?, scale?}?); $sphere(r, at, mat, scale?); $lathe([[r, y]...] from the axis out and back, [x, y, z] base, mat, {scale?, seg?}?); $rod(from, to, r, mat); $ring(R, r, at, mat, axis? y|z); $tube([[x, y, z]...], r, mat); $panel([x, z], [x, z], y, h, t, mat); $extrude(plane side (profile x,y across z) | plan (profile x,z upwards), [[x, y] | {"arc": [cx, cy, r, a0, a1, clockwise]} | {"curve": [cx, cy, x, y]}], depth, mat, bevel?, at?); $fourLegs(w, d, inset, h, r, mat, square?); $noplan(part or list) leaves parts out of the plan symbol; $base({structure, water, grate}) is an element's own geometry. The plan symbol is the parts seen from above. Keep parts inside the w × d footprint and under h. A part list ({ let, parts } from before scripts) is still accepted and converted.",
    "examples": [],
    "input": {
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "properties": {
        "for": {
          "items": {
            "description": "What a look draws: 'furniture:<type>' (a furniture type), 'opening:<door|sliding-door|gate|window|opening|glass-block>', 'element:<stair|pond|drain>' or 'wall:railing'",
            "pattern": "^(furniture|opening|element|wall):[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$",
            "type": "string",
          },
          "minItems": 1,
          "type": "array",
        },
        "name": {
          "description": "Name of the look, in the language the project data uses",
          "minLength": 1,
          "type": "string",
        },
        "recipe": {
          "description": "A recipe: { params?, seed?, script, materials? } with a JSONata script (see the recipe format in look_add)",
        },
        "use": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Use it for every object of these targets",
          "type": "boolean",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "name",
        "for",
        "recipe",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "kind": "edit",
    "name": "look_add",
    "permission": "reversible",
    "runtime": "any",
    "title": "Add a look",
  },
  {
    "description": "Change a look of the design: its name, the targets it fits or its recipe (format in look_add). Every object drawn with it changes.",
